course: dictionary
lesson: 5963
title: Mathematics and its short forms
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mathematics is the study of numbers, shapes, patterns and measurements, and of how they connect. At school it is also a subject, so when a teacher says «mathematics lesson», she means the lesson where you learn these things.
explain: Context helps here. «Uranus was found with a telescope in 1781, Neptune was predicted by mathematics and found in 1846» tells you that mathematics was used to work out where Neptune must be before anyone saw it. So mathematics is more than adding numbers: it is careful reasoning.
explain: Some words have short forms that people use in different places. «Britain, Ireland and Australia say "maths"; the USA and Canada say "math"; both are short for "mathematics".» The meaning stays the same. Only the spelling and the sound change.
fact: Mathematics means the study of numbers, quantities, shapes, patterns and measurements.
fact: At school, mathematics includes arithmetic, fractions, geometry, measures, statistics and algebra.
fact: «Mathematics» is an uncountable noun, so you cannot say "two mathematics".
fact: «Maths» is used in Britain, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand; «math» is used in the USA and Canada.
fact: «Lewis Carroll» was the pen name of Charles Dodgson, a mathematics teacher at Christ Church college in Oxford.
exercise: «Uranus was found with a telescope in 1781, Neptune was predicted by mathematics and found in 1846.» How was Neptune found? | Found by a teacher in Oxford | *Predicted by mathematics, then found | Found in 1930 from photographs | Found by chance with a camera
exercise: In Australia, which short form of mathematics do people often use? | *maths | mathe | mathics | math
exercise: What was Charles Dodgson's job at Christ Church college? | *A mathematics teacher | A cook | A doctor | A park keeper
test: Which of these is part of mathematics at school? | Singing songs | Painting pictures | *Fractions | Reading stories
test: In the sentence about Neptune, what helped astronomers find it before it was seen? | *mathematics | a dog | a cake | a whistle
exam: Is mathematics a noun you can count, like "two apples"? | Yes, you can say "two mathematics" | Only in the USA | *No, it is uncountable | Yes, it is always plural
